== bedrock init (hidden false)
Write a starting bedrock.yaml for a new app.
Write a starting bedrock.yaml for a new app.

The file is commented and ready to deploy once the app itself exists.
Nothing on a machine is touched; this only writes a file.

  bedrock init blog --kind static --host blog.example.com
  bedrock init api --host api.example.com --postgres
  bedrock init mailer --kind worker
  bedrock init nightly --kind cron --schedule "0 4 * * *"
Usage:
  bedrock init <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --dir string        the directory to write the manifest into (default ".")
      --force             overwrite an existing manifest
      --host string       the hostname it answers on, for web and static
      --kind string       what the app is: web, static, worker or cron (default "web")
      --port int          the port a web app listens on (default 8000)
      --postgres          give the app a database, and DATABASE_URL to reach it
      --schedule string   when a cron app runs (default "0 3 * * *")

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
