List the apps on this machine: owner, hosts, repository, last deploy, last backup.

Usage:
  bedrock ls [flags]

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
== bedrock move (hidden false)
Move an app, and its data, to another machine.
Move an app, and its data, to another machine.

A move is two commands, one on each machine, with the backup bucket as the
channel between them. Both machines must be pointed at the same storage: the
same bucket prefix and the same backup password. A machine reading its own
bucket instead of the source's finds nothing there, which looks like an app
with no data rather than like a failure.

  on the source:  bedrock move out <app> --yes > handoff.json
  on the target:  bedrock move in <app> --handoff handoff.json
                  bedrock deploy <source>
                  bedrock dns point <host>          # once it answers
  on the source:  bedrock remove <app>              # after a rollback window

The source keeps running and keeps serving throughout. Nothing is torn down
until you tear it down, which is what makes the window a real one.
Usage:
  bedrock move [command]

Available Commands:
  in          Restore an app's data here from the handoff the source machine wrote.
  out         Back an app up and write the handoff the target machine reads.
  secrets     Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")

Use "bedrock move [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== bedrock move in (hidden false)
Restore an app's data here from the handoff the source machine wrote.
Restore an app's data here from the handoff the source machine wrote.

This brings the data across and stops. Deploying the app is a separate
step, so the restore can be checked before anything starts serving, and
so the source keeps answering until you move DNS yourself.
Usage:
  bedrock move in <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --digest string    apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --handoff string   the handoff written by 'bedrock move out', or - for standard input (default "-")
      --plan             show the plan and change nothing
      --yes              apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
== bedrock move out (hidden false)
Back an app up and write the handoff the target machine reads.
Back an app up and write the handoff the target machine reads.

The backup is a maintenance window, so it is planned, asked about and
applied like any other operation; --digest names its plan. The handoff
goes to standard output alone and carries no secrets, so it can be
redirected to a file, read, and copied to the other machine.

The app is left running. A move is only safe because the source is still
there to go back to.
Usage:
  bedrock move out <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --digest string   apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --no-backup       use the last good backup instead of taking a fresh one
      --plan            show the plan and change nothing
      --yes             apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
== bedrock move secrets (hidden false)
Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.
Seal an app's secrets for the target machine, and accept them there.

A moved app has to arrive with the secrets it left with. Its database comes
back from a snapshot still holding the role password the source generated, so
a target that generated its own would be handed a database it cannot open.
bedrock refuses that restore rather than performing it, which is why this
step exists.

  on the target:  bedrock secret recipient
  on the source:  bedrock move secrets <app> --to <age1...> > secrets.age
  on the target:  bedrock move secrets <app> --accept < secrets.age

Only ciphertext is ever printed, sealed to the target's own key, and it stops
being readable ten minutes after it is made. The values never appear in a
terminal, a log or a shell history on either machine.
Usage:
  bedrock move secrets <app> [flags]

Flags:
      --accept      read a sealed bundle from standard input and store it here
      --to string   the target machine's recipient, from 'bedrock secret recipient' there

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
== bedrock preview (hidden false)
Run a branch of an app beside it, behind a sign-in.
Run a branch of an app beside it, behind a sign-in.

A preview is an ordinary app with a derived name, its own containers and its
own empty database, deployed from a branch. Pushing the same branch again
updates it rather than making a second one.

  bedrock preview up https://github.com/you/app --branch new-nav \
      --domain preview.example.com
  bedrock preview ls
  bedrock remove app-pr-new-nav --data

Every preview route is behind the Core's sign-in and there is no way to turn
that off, so the machine needs the loom integration before any of this works.
Usage:
  bedrock preview [command]

Available Commands:
  ls          List the previews running on this machine.
  up          Deploy a branch as a preview of the app in it.

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")

Use "bedrock preview [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== bedrock preview ls (hidden false)
List the previews running on this machine.

Usage:
  bedrock preview ls [flags]

Aliases:
  ls, list

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
== bedrock preview up (hidden false)
Deploy a branch as a preview of the app in it.
Deploy a branch as a preview of the app in it.

The branch's own bedrock.yaml says which app this is a preview of. The
preview is derived from it: a name with the branch in it, hostnames under
the preview domain, every route behind a sign-in, no checks and no backups.

The database starts empty and is built by whatever the app runs to migrate
itself. Most branches want a schema rather than somebody's real rows.

The preview's hostnames keep their records the way the app's own routes
say. An app whose records are kept by hand has none for a preview, so
either --dns direct makes them, or a wildcard under the preview domain
points at this machine already.
Usage:
  bedrock preview up <repository> [flags]

Flags:
      --branch string     the branch to preview
      --digest string     apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --dns string        keep the preview's records this way: direct, proxied or manual (default: as the app's routes say)
      --domain string     the domain previews answer under, such as preview.example.com
      --manifest string   the manifest at the branch's root, for a source that holds several apps (default "bedrock.yaml")
      --plan              show the plan and change nothing
      --secrets           copy the parent's hand-set secrets so the preview can start (default true)
      --yes               apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
