== bedrock upgrade (hidden false)
Replace bedrock with a new build and restart the daemon; systemd rolls back if it can't start.
Replace bedrock with a new build and restart the daemon; systemd rolls back if it can't start.

With --version the build is downloaded from the published release and checked
against the SHA256SUMS beside it. That proves the two agree and nothing more,
since whoever can replace one can replace both; pass --sha256 with a checksum
from reviewed source to pin it.

  bedrock upgrade --version 0.7.4
  bedrock upgrade /tmp/bedrock-new
Usage:
  bedrock upgrade [path-to-new-bedrock] [flags]

Flags:
      --digest string    apply only if the plan is still the one with this digest; implies --yes
      --plan             show the plan and change nothing
      --sha256 string    the expected checksum of that release's build for this machine
      --version string   a published release to fetch and install, such as 0.7.4
      --yes              apply without asking

Global Flags:
      --json               print JSON, one object per line
      --socket string      the daemon's socket; the local kernel is used when nothing answers (default "/run/bedrock/bedrock.sock")
      --state-dir string   where bedrock keeps its state (default "~/.bedrock")
